two members of the Navy's SEAL Team six  are under investigation this morning in  the death of an army green beret 34 year  old Staff Sergeant Logan Melgar was  found dead on June 4th he was reportedly  strangled in embassy housing in the  capital of Mali Navy investigators have  taken over the case to see if the seals  had any connection to his death David  Martin is at the Pentagon David good  morning good morning after weeks of  revelations about US military operations  in the African country of New Year  another operation is coming to light in  the neighboring country of Mali another  American soldier is dead only this time  it's a homicide army green beret Staff  Sergeant Logan Melgar died last June in  a house he shared with the two Navy  SEALs in the capital of Mali an autopsy  ruled his death a homicide reportedly by  asphyxiation a law enforcement source  says the two Navy SEALs had been  involved in an argument with him and are  now under investigation by the Naval  Criminal Investigative Service  neither the Pentagon nor the u.s. Africa  Command announced Sergeant Mel gars  death apparently because it would reveal  the presence of special operations  forces in Mali Sargent Melgar was a  member of the same Special Forces group  as the four Americans who were killed  while on patrol in the neighboring  country of Nazaire earlier this month  the Islamic state fighters who ambushed  and killed the 4 soldiers were based in  Mali and escaped back across the border  a Texas native Melgar was honored at his  high school's homecoming football game  earlier this month the Pentagon is still  struggling to explain the sequence of  events which led to the deaths of those  four soldiers in Nazaire now it must  explain the death of Sergeant Melgar as  well as what American special operations  forces are doing in Mali Gail thank you  very much David
 

